1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:01,810
A Docker container is not a

2
00:00:01,810 --> 00:00:03,340
tiny virtual machine.

3
00:00:03,340 --> 00:00:04,700
It's just a Linux process,

4
00:00:04,700 --> 00:00:06,154
wearing a disguise.

5
00:00:06,224 --> 00:00:07,784
A virtual machine boots a full

6
00:00:07,784 --> 00:00:09,784
guest operating system, with its own

7
00:00:09,784 --> 00:00:10,384
kernel.

8
00:00:10,384 --> 00:00:11,937
Heavy, and slow.

9
00:00:11,967 --> 00:00:13,687
A container skips all of that.

10
00:00:13,687 --> 00:00:16,247
It shares the host's kernel, and relies on

11
00:00:16,247 --> 00:00:17,868
three kernel features.

12
00:00:17,898 --> 00:00:19,178
One: namespaces.

13
00:00:19,178 --> 00:00:21,498
They control what the process can see.

14
00:00:21,498 --> 00:00:24,098
Inside, it thinks it's process ID one,

15
00:00:24,098 --> 00:00:26,318
with its own network and hostname.

16
00:00:26,348 --> 00:00:27,468
Two: cgroups.

17
00:00:27,468 --> 00:00:29,068
They limit what it can use.

18
00:00:29,068 --> 00:00:32,508
Half a CPU, five hundred megabytes of RAM.

19
00:00:32,508 --> 00:00:34,428
Exceed the memory, and the kernel

20
00:00:34,428 --> 00:00:35,258
kills it.

21
00:00:35,288 --> 00:00:37,168
Three: a layered filesystem.

22
00:00:37,168 --> 00:00:39,928
Your image is a stack of read-only layers,

23
00:00:39,928 --> 00:00:42,682
with one thin writable layer on top.

24
00:00:42,712 --> 00:00:43,592
Don't believe me?

25
00:00:43,592 --> 00:00:45,192
Run ps on the host.

26
00:00:45,192 --> 00:00:46,792
Your container is right there,

27
00:00:46,792 --> 00:00:48,562
an ordinary process.

28
00:00:48,592 --> 00:00:50,192
That's why containers start in a

29
00:00:50,192 --> 00:00:52,192
fraction of a second, and one server

30
00:00:52,192 --> 00:00:53,830
can run hundreds of them.

31
00:00:53,930 --> 00:00:55,570
A container isn't a machine.

32
00:00:55,570 --> 00:00:57,884
It's a process with boundaries.

